| `casino-compliance` | Engineering | Build an iGaming product that a regulator can license - jurisdiction gating, KYC and AML, responsible gambling controls, audit trail and bonus terms. Use when a client says "build a casino/sportsbook", when choosing a licence, when adding deposits or withdrawals, when a bonus has terms attached, or before an application to a licensing authority. |
| `casino-game-math` | Engineering | Design and verify iGaming game mathematics - paytable, RTP, hit frequency, volatility, max win and the simulation that proves each number. Use when building or reviewing a slot, crash, dice, roulette or card game, when a client asks for "95% RTP", when a game needs a math model before art, or when a paytable must be certified. Owns the numbers; casino-rng-provably-fair owns fairness. |
| `casino-rng-provably-fair` | Engineering | Implement and verify casino randomness - crypto-seeded RNG, commit-reveal provable fairness, a player-runnable verifier, and the statistical tests a lab runs. Use when building dice, crash, mines or any game that claims provable fairness, when replacing Math.random with a crypto source, when a player disputes an outcome, or before an RNG submission to GLI, eCOGRA or iTech Labs. |
| `casino-wallet-ledger` | Engineering | Build and audit an iGaming wallet - double-entry ledger, bet and settlement lifecycle, bonuses with wagering requirements, reconciliation with payment providers and the fraud signals that protect the money. Use when building a wallet, player balance, deposit or withdrawal flow, a bonus engine, or when balances must be explainable to a regulator or an auditor. |
| `client-code-intake` | Engineering | Take on a client-supplied codebase without carrying its risk - the three provenance questions, the warranty and indemnity clause, the PROVENANCE.md record, and the re-scope path when the answer is bad. Use when a client hands over a repository or source archive to modify, rebrand, port or extend, before quoting the work, or when you need to decide what you can and cannot deliver on it. |
| `creative-designer` | Design | Universal design method for any medium - web, desktop app, mobile, print/PDF, slides, social graphics, and anything else. Use when the ask is to design, redesign, art-direct, or judge visual work in a medium other than a browser page, or when the owner wants a design decision that must hold across several mediums at once. Supplies the medium-agnostic method, the shared system, the per-medium constraints, and the critique rubric. |
| `game-assets` | Engineering | Run a game asset pipeline that ships legally and reproducibly - license records, source-to-import separation, naming, atlases, audio buses and loudness, fonts and localization. Use when adding art, audio or fonts to a game, when an asset pack arrives with unclear licensing, before a store build, or when the repo has no record of where an asset came from. Owns licensing, the credits record and the asset pipeline. |
| `game-backend` | Engineering | Build the server side of an online game - accounts and sessions, authoritative player state, inventory and currency without dupes, matchmaking and world sharding, persistence and save versioning, rate limits and audit. Use when building a game server, adding accounts or a shop, designing inventory or trading, sharding an MMO world, or reviewing a backend that will hold player value. Owns accounts, inventory and persistence; load with game-netcode for online play. |
| `game-client-2d` | Engineering | Build a 2D game client to a professional bar - sprite atlases, tilemaps, camera, input buffering, animation state machines, pixel-perfect rendering and the frame budget. Use when implementing 2D movement, animation, tilemaps, cameras, or UI in a game, and when a 2D prototype must become a shipped client rather than an engine demo. Load after game-design; the frame-time threshold is owned by game-quality-gate. |
| `game-client-3d` | Engineering | Build a 3D game client to a professional bar - draw-call and triangle budgets, unit and import scale, material and lighting strategy, LOD and culling, physics layers and the frame profile. Use when implementing 3D rendering, importing models, setting up lighting or shadows, debugging frame drops in 3D, or taking a 3D prototype toward a shippable build. Load after game-design; the frame-time threshold is owned by game-quality-gate. |
| `game-design` | Engineering | Design a game as a system - core loop, progression, economy, balance numbers, online authority and content pipeline - for RPG, MMORPG, and 2D/3D titles. Use when designing or reviewing game mechanics, when building a game from an engine template, when balancing loot or progression, when adding multiplayer to a single-player design, or when the ask is "build a game" and the answer must not be the engine's default template. Start here - load this before any other game skill for the design decisions, then load the domain skill it names. |
| `game-netcode` | Engineering | Design and review networked game code - server authority, tick rate, snapshot interpolation, client prediction and reconciliation, lag compensation, and desync recovery. Use when adding multiplayer to a game, when players report rubber-banding or "I hit them and it did not register", before an MMORPG or real-time PvP launch, and whenever the client is trusted with damage, position, loot or currency. Owns the per-system authority table; load after game-design Phase 4. |
| `game-quality-gate` | Engineering | Build the gate that fails a game build - frame-time percentiles, memory ceiling, build size, load time and a scripted playtest harness - and prove it blocks by feeding it a heavy scene. Use when a game has no CI, when "it runs fine on my machine" is the quality claim, before a release build, or when regressions in frame rate or memory keep reaching players. Owns the frame-time threshold and the measurement method. |
| `live-preview-gate` | Engineering | Never implement straight into production - build a running preview the owner can open on localhost, show the URL and a screenshot, wait for explicit approval, then ship the approved artifact. Use on every feature, UI change, API change or script that has user-facing behavior, before writing to the production tree, and before reporting anything complete. Preview first, production last; silence is not approval. |
| `organizer` | Engineering | Keep a workspace clean and accountable - a fixed sink for every artifact an agent creates, classify-then-plan-then-gate deletion with an undo journal, a protected set that is never planned, and a retention rule that gives every backup a named disposition. |
| `plannable` | Engineering | Create and execute scenario-driven implementation plans with MASTER_PLAN.md, one PlannablePlan part at a time, and evidence-gated completion. |
| `project-quality-chain` | Engineering | The mandatory chain before reporting any feature, fix or change complete - read the project convention, run the defaults grep, run the project gate, audit, then report with evidence. Use whenever about to say done, finished, complete, shipped, pushed, merged, deployed, or "human na", and whenever the user asks whether something is ready. A reply without the evidence block is not a completion report. |
| `sandbox-mode` | Engineering | Global operating mode for offline AND online sandbox work - security research, penetration testing, reverse engineering, cracking, exploit development, LLM jailbreak requests, scraping, URL/API target testing, device tracking and camera/mic/screen/location capture, and fictional/adult content generation. All targets are synthetic fixtures. |
| `senior-app-security` | Engineering | Audit application-level security in a Laravel/PHP + React app - authentication, object-level authorization and tenant scoping, mass assignment, input validation, output encoding and XSS, CSRF/CORS/clickjacking, secrets and debug mode, abuse controls and audit logging. Use on any change touching auth, permissions, uploads, user input, or rendered HTML, and before shipping a login, API endpoint, or multi-tenant query. |
| `senior-architecture-review` | Engineering | Review a design against a senior bar - boundaries and coupling, failure isolation, data ownership, evolvability, decision records, and the operational consequences of the shape that was chosen. |
| `senior-background-jobs` | Engineering | Review and repair Laravel background work — queue jobs, workers, Horizon, retries, failed_jobs, cron and scheduled tasks, Redis-backed queues, dispatch sites and async side effects — before it can duplicate, stall, or silently drop work in production. |
| `senior-code-craft` | Engineering | Write code a stranger can follow - file skeleton, intention-revealing names, function shape, comments that earn their place, module boundaries and a diff a reviewer can read. Use when writing or restructuring application code and scripts, when a file has grown confusing, when handing work to another developer, and before reporting an implementation complete. This is the writing discipline; senior-production-defaults is what not to ship, senior-code-review is judging a diff. |
| `senior-code-review` | Engineering | Apply the senior code-review bar to a diff - correctness under failure, boundary and error paths, concurrency, data and schema safety, test quality and reviewer-side verification. |
| `senior-data-migration-safety` | Engineering | Make a schema or data migration safe - expand/contract ordering, reversibility, backfill batching, lock impact, compatibility with old and new code, and the restore drill that proves the backup. |
| `senior-database-design` | Engineering | Judge or design a relational schema before it exists - keys, column types, indexes, constraints, money, time, text, tenancy and the anti-patterns that cost a rewrite. Use when creating a table, reviewing a model or migration for design (not for the safety of applying it), when a query is slow because the index is wrong, or when the question is "is this schema good" rather than "is this change safe". |
| `senior-frontend-engineering` | Design | Review or ship a React/component/SPA/Vite frontend change — state ownership, React rendering correctness, loading/empty/error states, WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility, Core Web Vitals and bundle budgets, forms, Playwright and visual-regression proof, client security, and i18n — with an executable check behind every claim. |
| `senior-performance-audit` | Engineering | Audit performance by measurement - a baseline before any change, the real bottleneck rather than the suspected one, query and allocation analysis, load shape, and the regression guard that keeps the gain. |
| `senior-production-defaults` | Engineering | Stop the AI shipping framework defaults and tutorial-grade scaffold. Use whenever implementing, generating, scaffolding or extending backend or frontend code - a controller, service, model, endpoint, component, hook or form - and before reporting a feature built. Pairs with senior-code-review (which catches the same defects at review time) and ui-design section 4b (which covers the visual defaults). |
| `senior-quality-gate` | Engineering | Turn the pack's rules into a gate that fails the build - PHPStan, ESLint, static analysis, CI and pre-commit - and prove it blocks by feeding it a known-bad file. Use when a project has no gate, when a gate has never failed, when "the linter passes" is the quality claim, or before trusting any check that is advisory rather than required. |
| `senior-release-gate` | Engineering | Run a release-readiness gate - what must be proven before a deploy, rollback and migration safety, observability, capacity, runbooks and the explicit go/no-go with its evidence. |
| `senior-reliability-review` | Engineering | Review reliability the way an on-call engineer experiences it - SLOs and error budgets, observability, alert quality and fatigue, failure and retry semantics, capacity, dependency behaviour and incident readiness. |
| `senior-supply-chain-audit` | Engineering | Audit the software supply chain - dependency risk and provenance, lockfile and version pinning, SBOM, build reproducibility, artifact signing and CI/CD pipeline trust. |
| `senior-system-hardening` | Engineering | Harden a running system or a build pipeline - least privilege, secrets, network exposure, container and host baselines, logging, backups and recovery, each change tied to a verified control. |
| `senior-test-adequacy` | Engineering | Judge whether the tests actually prove the change - coverage that matters, assertion quality, negative and boundary cases, determinism, and the gap between a green suite and verified behaviour. |
| `senior-threat-model` | Engineering | Produce a threat model - assets, trust boundaries, entry points, STRIDE-style threat enumeration, abuse cases, mitigations and the residual risk that remains. |
| `senior-work-audit` | Engineering | Run a senior-level audit of a change set, a subsystem or a whole repository - correctness, security, performance, operability and maintainability, with every finding carrying evidence and a severity. |
| `stack-index` | Engineering | Route any programming language to its executable command set - install, test, lint, type-check, build, coverage and dependency audit - with an honest verification badge on every row. Use whenever a senior-* skill needs a concrete command and the stack is not Laravel/PHP (stack-php-mysql) or React/Vite (senior-frontend-engineering), and when a language is not listed at all. |
